// DEPENDS ON A DEFECT, AND MUST MIGRATE WHEN IT IS FIXED (2026-08-03).
//
// The spelling below reads a column an EARLIER entry of the same block wrote.
// That works today only because the implementation lands entries in written
// order, which is a bug against the design: a `stored` block is ONE write, and
// every rhs reads pre-state (`frag-std-store-design` — "Writes interleave,
// never overlap"; 690_126 pins the correct behaviour red).
//
// This test is green for the wrong reason. When the write path is fixed it goes
// red, and the migration is a CHAIN of two blocks — compute the staged column
// in the first, consume it in the second — which is one traversal and should
// cost nothing once the write path stops emitting an ordered call per field.
// Do not add a third test in this shape.
//
// PINS: heavy_compute's ITERATION shape — a frame loop (for wrapping sweep,
// 690_120's nesting) re-inverting the SAME matrix in place each pass:
// sixteen inverse entries into staging columns, then sixteen copy-backs, all
// one stored block, legal because later entries read earlier writes (690_126).
// Two passes: inverse of the inverse, so the store lands back on the exact
// integer matrix it started from — a value that only survives both passes
// being complete and ordered.
//
// The matrix is dense with det 2; its inverse has no zero entries, so no
// signed-zero formatting enters the expected text.
import std/io
import std/store

std/store:new(mats, capacity: 2) { t00: f64, t01: f64, t02: f64, t03: f64, t10: f64, t11: f64, t12: f64, t13: f64, t20: f64, t21: f64, t22: f64, t23: f64, t30: f64, t31: f64, t32: f64, t33: f64, o00: f64, o01: f64, o02: f64, o03: f64, o10: f64, o11: f64, o12: f64, o13: f64, o20: f64, o21: f64, o22: f64, o23: f64, o30: f64, o31: f64, o32: f64, o33: f64 }

std/store:insert(mats) { t00: -3.0, t01: -3.0, t02: 2.0, t03: -3.0, t10: 2.0, t11: -3.0, t12: -1.0, t13: -3.0, t20: -1.0, t21: -1.0, t22: 2.0, t23: 2.0, t30: 1.0, t31: 2.0, t32: -3.0, t33: -3.0, o00: 0.0, o01: 0.0, o02: 0.0, o03: 0.0, o10: 0.0, o11: 0.0, o12: 0.0, o13: 0.0, o20: 0.0, o21: 0.0, o22: 0.0, o23: 0.0, o30: 0.0, o31: 0.0, o32: 0.0, o33: 0.0 }

